checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-stranger-page.txt:14:USA Today headlined the release “September jobs report shows slowdown in hiring, unemployment ticks up”. The bureau says an over-the-month employment change of about 122,000 is statistically significant in the establishment survey. September's estimate of 29,000 is under that bar, and the bureau's own summary is that payrolls “changed little”. The 29,000 is a net change in payroll jobs; hires are a separate count of “all additions to the payroll” during the month, and September's has not been published.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-stranger-page.txt:16:A St. Louis Fed note published October 2 gives the rate without rounding: it “increased slightly from 4.141% to 4.175%”, so most of the visible tenth is rounding.
checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-stranger-page.txt:53:In the establishment survey, estimates for the most recent two months are labeled preliminary because they are based on incomplete returns. September's count of hires comes with the Job Openings and Labor Turnover release on November 3. The newest count is August's, when hires changed little at 5.2 million.
src/pages/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.astro:61:          <p>July was revised down by 31,000, from a gain of 21,000 to a loss of 10,000. August was revised down by 29,000, from a gain of 162,000 to a gain of 133,000. The August release had reported a gain of 162,000. The two months together are 60,000 lower than previously reported.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="employment in July and August combined is 60,000 lower than previously reported" /><Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-08" passage="Total nonfarm payroll employment increased by 162,000 in August, and the unemployment rate was unchanged at 4.1 percent" /></p>
src/pages/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.astro:107:        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">USA Today headlined the release &ldquo;September jobs report shows slowdown in hiring, unemployment ticks up&rdquo;.<Cite s="usatoday-september-jobs" passage="September jobs report shows slowdown in hiring, unemployment ticks up" /> The bureau says an over-the-month employment change of about {jobs(establishment.significanceThreshold * 1000)} is statistically significant in the establishment survey.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="An over-the-month employment change of about 122,000 is statistically significant in the establishment survey" /> September's estimate of {jobs(septemberPayrollChange * 1000)} is under that bar, and the bureau's own summary is that payrolls &ldquo;changed little&rdquo;.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="Total nonfarm payroll employment changed little in September (+29,000)" /> The {jobs(septemberPayrollChange * 1000)} is a net change in payroll jobs; hires are a separate count of &ldquo;all additions to the payroll&rdquo; during the month, and September's has not been published.<Cite s="bls-jolts-2026-08" passage="all additions to the payroll" /><Cite s="bls-jolts-2026-08" passage="the net employment change from the much larger CES survey" /><Cite s="bls-jolts-2026-08" passage="The Job Openings and Labor Turnover news release for September 2026 is scheduled to be published on Tuesday, November 3, 2026, at 10:00 a.m. (ET)." /></p>
src/pages/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.astro:109:        <p data-layer="narrative">A St. Louis Fed note published October 2 gives the rate without rounding: it &ldquo;increased slightly from 4.141% to 4.175%&rdquo;, so most of the visible tenth is rounding.<Cite s="stlouisfed-unrate-2026-10" passage="St. Louis Fed On the Economy, Oct. 2, 2026." /><Cite s="stlouisfed-unrate-2026-10" passage="More precise data show the rate increased slightly from 4.141% to 4.175%." /></p>
src/pages/events/jobs/2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.astro:135:        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="6">In the establishment survey, estimates for the most recent two months are labeled preliminary because they are based on incomplete returns.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-09" passage="estimates for the most recent 2 months are based on incomplete returns; for this reason, these estimates are labeled preliminary" /> September's count of hires comes with the Job Openings and Labor Turnover release on November 3. The newest count is August's, when hires changed little at {admitted.joltsHiresMillions} million.<Cite s="bls-jolts-2026-08" passage="Hires changed little at 5.2 million" /><Cite s="bls-jolts-2026-08" passage="The Job Openings and Labor Turnover news release for September 2026 is scheduled to be published on Tuesday, November 3, 2026, at 10:00 a.m. (ET)." /></p>

